  "worked_solution": "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $\u2207\\cdot (kF)=k\u2207\\cdot F$\n\n- $\u2207\\cdot (F+G)=\u2207\\cdot F+\u2207\\cdot G.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207\\cdot (3F)=3\u2207\\cdot F.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $\u2207\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207\\cdot F & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(2yx+{x}^{2})+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(5-{y}^{3})+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202z}(-2yz) \\\\  & =2y+2x-3{y}^{2}-2y \\\\  & =2x-3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207\\cdot (3F) & =3\u2207\\cdot F \\\\  & =3(2x-3{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =6x-9{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
